18:["$","div",null,{"className":"article-introduction","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"article-margin-label","children":"The question"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Pair each point of one planar set with a point of another, so that the total squared distance is as small as possible. Now let one set turn about its centre. As it turns, the best pairing changes. How often?"}]]}]
19:["$","figure",null,{"id":"film","className":"article-film","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"article-film-bar","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"01 / Research film"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"02:20 · Sound on"}]]}],["$","video",null,{"controls":true,"playsInline":true,"preload":"none","width":"1920","height":"1080","poster":"/media/articles/planar-matching/v1/poster.jpg","aria-label":"Planar matching under rotation, narrated research film","aria-describedby":"film-clarification","children":[["$","source",null,{"src":"/media/articles/planar-matching/v1/film.mp4","type":"video/mp4"}],["$","track",null,{"kind":"captions","srcLang":"en","label":"English","src":"/media/articles/planar-matching/v1/captions.en.vtt"}],"Your browser cannot play this film. ",["$","a",null,{"href":"/media/articles/planar-matching/v1/film.mp4","children":"Open the MP4"}]," or read the transcript below."]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"A narrated introduction · English captions and transcript"}],["$","a",null,{"href":"#film-transcript","children":"Read transcript ↓"}]]}],["$","p",null,{"id":"film-clarification","className":"film-clarification","children":"In the film, “best pairings” means pairings that are uniquely optimal at some angle, not every pairing in a tie. The three-dimensional example establishes at least thirteen. The polynomial-time algorithm assumes rational input coordinates."}]]}]
1a:["$","div",null,{"className":"article-reading-grid","children":[["$","aside",null,{"className":"article-contents","aria-label":"On this page","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"article-margin-label","children":"In this article"}],["$","nav",null,{"children":[[["$","a","question",{"href":"#question","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"01"}],"A small answer to a large search"]}],["$","a","polygon",{"href":"#polygon","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"02"}],"A change of picture"]}],["$","a","sharpness",{"href":"#sharpness","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"03"}],"The simplest arrangement is already extreme"]}],["$","a","winding",{"href":"#winding","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"04"}],"Where the count comes from"]}],["$","a","dimension",{"href":"#dimension","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"05"}],"Why the plane matters"]}],["$","a","alignment",{"href":"#alignment","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"06"}],"From counting to alignment"]}]],["$","a",null,{"href":"#research-materials","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"↗"}],"Paper & verification"]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"article-bound","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"At most"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"n(n − 1)"}],["$","span",null,{"children":["unique optima",["$","br",null,{}],"one planar turn · n ≥ 2"]}]]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"article-prose","children":[[["$","section","question",{"id":"question","aria-labelledby":"question-title","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"article-section-number","children":["01"," /"]}],["$","h2",null,{"id":"question-title","children":"A small answer to a large search"}],[["$","p","Günter Rote presented the rotational counting question in 2008, tracing it back to Karel Zikan in 1991. There are n! possible pairings: for ten points, more than three and a half million. The sharp bound proved in the accompanying manuscript is much smaller. For n ≥ 2, at most n(n − 1) different pairings can be uniquely optimal during one full turn. For ten points, that is ninety.",{"children":"Günter Rote presented the rotational counting question in 2008, tracing it back to Karel Zikan in 1991. There are n! possible pairings: for ten points, more than three and a half million. The sharp bound proved in the accompanying manuscript is much smaller. For n ≥ 2, at most n(n − 1) different pairings can be uniquely optimal during one full turn. For ten points, that is ninety."}],["$","p","The word “uniquely” matters. Counting all pairings involved in a tie can give n! even without any motion. The paper also gives a specified tie-breaking rule with a bounded number of selected pairings; arbitrary tie-breaking does not have that guarantee.",{"children":"The word “uniquely” matters. Counting all pairings involved in a tie can give n! even without any motion. The paper also gives a specified tie-breaking rule with a bounded number of selected pairings; arbitrary tie-breaking does not have that guarantee."}]]]}],["$","section","polygon",{"id":"polygon","aria-labelledby":"polygon-title","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"article-section-number","children":["02"," /"]}],["$","h2",null,{"id":"polygon-title","children":"A change of picture"}],[["$","p","The argument starts by replacing each pairing with a single point in a new plane. Its two coordinates record how well the pairing aligns the sets along two perpendicular rotation phases. As one set turns, choosing the best pairing becomes choosing the farthest point of this cloud in a sweeping direction.",{"children":"The argument starts by replacing each pairing with a single point in a new plane. Its two coordinates record how well the pairing aligns the sets along two perpendicular rotation phases. As one set turns, choosing the best pairing becomes choosing the farthest point of this cloud in a sweeping direction."}],"$L1d"]]}],"$L1e","$L1f","$L20","$L21"],"$L22","$L23"]}]]}]
