1d:["$","p","Only corners of the cloud’s convex hull can be strict winners. A corner may represent several tied pairings, and an entire edge may be optimal at a transition. Even with such degeneracies, the polygon has at most n(n − 1) distinct vertices. The factorial cloud has a quadratic outline.",{"children":"Only corners of the cloud’s convex hull can be strict winners. A corner may represent several tied pairings, and an entire edge may be optimal at a transition. Even with such degeneracies, the polygon has at most n(n − 1) distinct vertices. The factorial cloud has a quadratic outline."}]
1e:["$","section","sharpness",{"id":"sharpness","aria-labelledby":"sharpness-title","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"article-section-number","children":["03"," /"]}],["$","h2",null,{"id":"sharpness-title","children":"The simplest arrangement is already extreme"}],[["$","p","Place the fixed points at distinct positions on a line. The best pairing sorts the moving points by their shadows on that line. Each pair of distinct moving points exchanges projection order twice per turn.",{"children":"Place the fixed points at distinct positions on a line. The best pairing sorts the moving points by their shadows on that line. Each pair of distinct moving points exchanges projection order twice per turn."}],["$","p","Choose the moving points so that no two of these exchange times coincide. Then the n(n − 1) exchanges produce that many distinct strict projection orders, each with its own uniquely optimal pairing. This is an existence construction, not a claim that every collinear arrangement attains the bound. For n ≥ 3, small perturbations preserve all the strict winners, so both sets can be noncollinear and still reach the same limit.",{"children":"Choose the moving points so that no two of these exchange times coincide. Then the n(n − 1) exchanges produce that many distinct strict projection orders, each with its own uniquely optimal pairing. This is an existence construction, not a claim that every collinear arrangement attains the bound. For n ≥ 3, small perturbations preserve all the strict winners, so both sets can be noncollinear and still reach the same limit."}]]]}]
1f:["$","section","winding",{"id":"winding","aria-labelledby":"winding-title","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"article-section-number","children":["04"," /"]}],["$","h2",null,{"id":"winding-title","children":"Where the count comes from"}],[["$","p","During one full rotation, every pair of moving points winds once. There are n(n − 1)/2 pairs: together they contribute n(n − 1) half-turns. The proof shows why this total limits how often the optimum can switch.",{"children":"During one full rotation, every pair of moving points winds once. There are n(n − 1)/2 pairs: together they contribute n(n − 1) half-turns. The proof shows why this total limits how often the optimum can switch."}],["$","p","First consider generic data, where switches are isolated. Relabel the moving points by their current partners, and connect the relabelled configurations across each switch using the proof’s small collision-free bridges. The resulting loop stays in a convex region where one fixed pairing is strictly optimal. Inside that region there is no collision to wind around, so the loop’s total winding is zero.",{"children":"First consider generic data, where switches are isolated. Relabel the moving points by their current partners, and connect the relabelled configurations across each switch using the proof’s small collision-free bridges. The resulting loop stays in a convex region where one fixed pairing is strictly optimal. Inside that region there is no collision to wind around, so the loop’s total winding is zero."}],["$","p","The bridges therefore cancel the physical winding. Each switch contributes a positive integer charge, measured in half-turns, of at least one. Their total is n(n − 1), so there cannot be more switches than that. Longer cycles can consume more than one unit. Perturbation then transfers the bound on distinct strict winners to nongeneric inputs.",{"children":"The bridges therefore cancel the physical winding. Each switch contributes a positive integer charge, measured in half-turns, of at least one. Their total is n(n − 1), so there cannot be more switches than that. Longer cycles can consume more than one unit. Perturbation then transfers the bound on distinct strict winners to nongeneric inputs."}],["$","p","The four-point illustration uses twelve half-turns and twelve switches of charge one. The general theorem goes further than rigid rotation: collision-free periodic motions with monotone pairwise angles are controlled by their total pair winding.",{"children":"The four-point illustration uses twelve half-turns and twelve switches of charge one. The general theorem goes further than rigid rotation: collision-free periodic motions with monotone pairwise angles are controlled by their total pair winding."}]]]}]
20:["$","section","dimension",{"id":"dimension","aria-labelledby":"dimension-title","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"article-section-number","children":["05"," /"]}],["$","h2",null,{"id":"dimension-title","children":"Why the plane matters"}],[["$","p","In three dimensions, a direction can move around on a sphere instead of winding around a circle. The planar counting argument no longer applies. That alone would not disprove the bound, but an explicit counterexample does: four points rotating about a fixed axis have at least thirteen distinct uniquely optimal pairings, exceeding the planar limit of twelve.",{"children":"In three dimensions, a direction can move around on a sphere instead of winding around a circle. The planar counting argument no longer applies. That alone would not disprove the bound, but an explicit counterexample does: four points rotating about a fixed axis have at least thirteen distinct uniquely optimal pairings, exceeding the planar limit of twelve."}],["$","p","The paper certifies thirteen strict winners at exact rational rotations. It does not claim that thirteen is the exact total, or settle the growth rate of the three-dimensional problem.",{"children":"The paper certifies thirteen strict winners at exact rational rotations. It does not claim that thirteen is the exact total, or settle the growth rate of the three-dimensional problem."}]]]}]
21:["$","section","alignment",{"id":"alignment","aria-labelledby":"alignment-title","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"article-section-number","children":["06"," /"]}],["$","h2",null,{"id":"alignment-title","children":"From counting to alignment"}],[["$","p","Once the correlation polygon is known, exact alignment is simple to express: choose a vertex farthest from the origin and recover its best rotation and translation. For rational planar coordinates, the paper enumerates the polygon in polynomial time using at most 2V exact assignment queries, where V ≤ n(n − 1). It is not necessary to enumerate all n! pairings.",{"children":"Once the correlation polygon is known, exact alignment is simple to express: choose a vertex farthest from the origin and recover its best rotation and translation. For rational planar coordinates, the paper enumerates the polygon in polynomial time using at most 2V exact assignment queries, where V ≤ n(n − 1). It is not necessary to enumerate all n! pairings."}],["$","p","This gives a globally optimal orientation-preserving rigid alignment and a realizing pairing. A separate branch handles reflections. The contribution is the worst-case bound and its algorithmic guarantee; exact registration methods themselves predate this work.",{"children":"This gives a globally optimal orientation-preserving rigid alignment and a realizing pairing. A separate branch handles reflections. The contribution is the worst-case bound and its algorithmic guarantee; exact registration methods themselves predate this work."}]]]}]
22:["$","details",null,{"id":"film-transcript","className":"article-transcript","children":[["$","summary",null,{"children":["Film transcript ",["$","span",null,{"children":"Original narration"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","p","Two sets of points lie in a plane. Pair them up, one to one, so that the total squared distance between partners is as small as possible. Now let one set turn about its centre. As it turns, the best pairing changes.",{"children":"Two sets of points lie in a plane. Pair them up, one to one, so that the total squared distance between partners is as small as possible. Now let one set turn about its centre. As it turns, the best pairing changes."}],["$","p","In 2008, the mathematician Günter Rote asked: during one full turn, how many different best pairings can there be? The number of pairings grows like n factorial. For ten points, that is more than three and a half million.",{"children":"In 2008, the mathematician Günter Rote asked: during one full turn, how many different best pairings can there be? The number of pairings grows like n factorial. For ten points, that is more than three and a half million."}],["$","p","The key is a change of picture. Each pairing becomes a single point in the plane. Turning the set sweeps a direction around this cloud, and the best pairing is always the point farthest in that direction. So only corners of the outline can ever win. Here, twelve of the twenty-four.",{"children":"The key is a change of picture. Each pairing becomes a single point in the plane. Turning the set sweeps a direction around this cloud, and the best pairing is always the point farthest in that direction. So only corners of the outline can ever win. Here, twelve of the twenty-four."}],["$","p","The answer: the outline never has more than n times n minus one corners, wherever the points are placed. For ten points, ninety.",{"children":"The answer: the outline never has more than n times n minus one corners, wherever the points are placed. For ten points, ninety."}],["$","p","Nothing smaller would do. Put the fixed points on a line, and the best pairing simply sorts the moving points by their shadows. Each pair of shadows changes order twice per turn. Six pairs, twelve changes: the simplest arrangement is already the extreme one.",{"children":"Nothing smaller would do. Put the fixed points on a line, and the best pairing simply sorts the moving points by their shadows. Each pair of shadows changes order twice per turn. Six pairs, twelve changes: the simplest arrangement is already the extreme one."}],["$","p","The count also has a reason. In one turn, every pair of moving points turns once around. Six pairs: twelve half-turns. Follow the points by their current partners. That arrangement always stays where its pairing is optimal, a region with no hole to wind around. So all the turning must be given back by switches, and each switch gives back at least half a turn. Twelve half-turns, twelve switches. The account balances.",{"children":"The count also has a reason. In one turn, every pair of moving points turns once around. Six pairs: twelve half-turns. Follow the points by their current partners. That arrangement always stays where its pairing is optimal, a region with no hole to wind around. So all the turning must be given back by switches, and each switch gives back at least half a turn. Twelve half-turns, twelve switches. The account balances."}],"$L24","$L25"]}]]}]
23:["$","section",null,{"className":"article-sources","aria-labelledby":"article-sources-title","children":[["$","h2",null,{"id":"article-sources-title","children":"Sources & production"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"AI-authored exposition and film. The film uses a synthetic voice (Kokoro), with music and sound generated for it. The transcript preserves the narration; the text above states the mathematical qualifications. No human verification or peer review is claimed."}],["$","p",null,{"children":"The mathematical account follows the manuscript below: Theorems 3.1 and 3.6, Proposition 5.2, and Appendix A. The film and this exposition are not themselves Lean-verified."}],["$","ul",null,{"children":[["$","li","https://ems.press/content/serial-article-files/46191#page=70",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"https://ems.press/content/serial-article-files/46191#page=70","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer","children":["Günter Rote · Least-squares matching of point sets under rotation · Oberwolfach Report 44/2008, pp. 2546–2547"," ↗"]}]}]]}]]}]
24:["$","p","The reason is planar, and so is the result. In space, directions can travel without winding, and four points turning about an axis give thirteen best pairings, one more than the plane allows.",{"children":"The reason is planar, and so is the result. In space, directions can travel without winding, and four points turning about an axis give thirteen best pairings, one more than the plane allows."}]
25:["$","p","One practical consequence: the best rigid alignment of two planar point sets with unknown correspondence can be computed exactly, by checking at most n times n minus one candidates.",{"children":"One practical consequence: the best rigid alignment of two planar point sets with unknown correspondence can be computed exactly, by checking at most n times n minus one candidates."}]
